---
import Answer from '../components/Answer.astro';
import { answers } from '../data/answers';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { OPERATOR_NAME, OWNER_ROLE, CONTACT_EMAIL, abs } from '../config';
const jsonld = [{ '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'Person', name: OPERATOR_NAME, jobTitle: OWNER_ROLE, url: abs('/about/') }];
---
<Base title="About ImageTo20KB" description="About ImageTo20KB, a browser-based image compressor for target file sizes such as 20KB and 100KB." path="/about/" jsonld={jsonld} crumbs={[{ name: 'About', href: '/about/' }]}>
  <div class="wrap"><article class="prose">
    <h1>About ImageTo20KB</h1>
    <Answer text={answers['about']} />
    <p>ImageTo20KB helps people reduce image files to a size limit, such as 20KB or 100KB, using their own browser.</p>
    <h2>Editorial and owner information</h2>
    <p><strong>{OPERATOR_NAME}, {OWNER_ROLE}</strong>, develops, edits and manages ImageTo20KB. He builds the compression tool and the website, writes and reviews the guides and blog posts, looks after hosting and updates, and answers email sent to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.</p>
    <h3>How content is written and checked</h3>
    <p>Method guides, such as how to photograph a signature or choose an image format, are written from how the tool behaves and from common upload problems. Size ranges for government exams are different: they change with each notification, so the exam pages present them as commonly reported figures, show the date they were last reviewed, and tell readers to confirm them on the official portal. We do not copy official notices.</p>
    <h3>Independence</h3>
    <p>The site currently carries no advertising and no sponsored content, and it is not affiliated with any exam body, government department, employer or university mentioned in the guides. If that ever changes, it will be stated clearly on the site.</p>
    <h3>Corrections</h3>
    <p>If you find a mistake or an out-of-date figure, email the address above with the page and the correction. Errors are fixed as soon as they are confirmed.</p>
    <h2>Why it exists</h2>
    <p>Online forms, exam portals, job sites and content systems often set strict upload limits in kilobytes. People then lose time trying different settings in photo editors or apps. ImageTo20KB turns the question around: you enter the size you need, and the tool searches for the best image it can make within it.</p>
    <h2>What we focus on</h2>
    <p>The main job is simple: compress image to 20KB, 100KB, 200KB or another target, entirely in your browser. We also provide an image resizer for maximum pixel dimensions and guides that explain how to prepare photos and signatures for forms. The tool reports honestly when a target cannot be reached rather than claiming success.</p>
    <h2>How we approach privacy</h2>
    <p>Compression runs entirely on your device. There is no upload step for compression and no external image-processing service. The site is a static web application hosted on Cloudflare's edge network, meaning there is no backend server or database storing your uploaded photos or signatures. You can review the exact operational details, including Cloudflare's network-level telemetry, on our <a href="/privacy-policy/">privacy policy</a> page. We do not make sweeping claims of absolute security; instead, we outline our exact technical controls and operational boundaries on the <a href="/security/">security page</a>.</p>
    <h2>Engineering origins and core philosophy</h2>
    <p>ImageTo20KB was created to solve a widespread, frustrating problem encountered daily by students, job seekers, and applicants worldwide: strict kilobyte upload restrictions on web forms. Government recruitment boards, academic institutions, and corporate application tracking systems routinely reject files that exceed arbitrary limits like 20KB or 50KB. When candidates attempt to resize these photos using general-purpose photo editors or messaging apps, the images frequently become either illegibly blurry or remain slightly above the upload cap.</p>
    <p>Our engineering goal was simple: replace trial-and-error slider guessing with deterministic client-side calculation. By letting candidates input their required kilobyte limit directly, our binary search algorithm pinpoints the optimum combination of canvas dimensions and compression quality in milliseconds. Most importantly, by keeping every stage of processing inside the client's browser, applicants can safely process sensitive documents—including passport photographs, thumb impressions, and legal signatures—without exposing personal biometric records to third-party cloud servers.</p>
    <h2>How we approach quality and visual fidelity</h2>
    <p>Aggressive JPEG compression introduces blocky artifacts, color discoloration, and muddy contours that can cause automated facial recognition software to reject an application. To prevent this, our engine favors moderate dimensional downscaling rather than extreme compression degradation. By maintaining reasonable visual quality thresholds, facial features stay sharp and signatures retain clean, unbroken lines.</p>
    <p>Users maintain complete control through our Advanced settings panel: you can define maximum bounding widths, establish minimum compression quality floors, or force original dimensions to be preserved. We rigorously test our algorithms across real-world JPEG, PNG, and WebP encoders to ensure our reported file sizes reflect actual disk usage.</p>
    <h2>Editorial transparency and independent testing</h2>
    <p>All instructional articles, format guides, and troubleshooting walkthroughs published on ImageTo20KB are written from hands-on software development experience and empirical testing against live recruitment portals. We routinely monitor and update our portal guidelines to reflect changes in official recruitment specifications.</p>
    <p>Because government exam bodies and private portals periodically revise their upload parameters without prior notice, we clearly display review dates on all exam guides and explicitly instruct users to cross-reference their official notification PDFs. We maintain strict editorial independence: we do not accept sponsored content, we do not promote commercial software, and we maintain no financial affiliation with any exam board, employer, or third-party service mentioned.</p>
    <h2>What we do not claim</h2>
    <p>We are not affiliated with, endorsed by, or partnered with any government ministry, recruitment agency, academic institution, or exam conducting board. While our tool meticulously optimizes files to match reported guidelines, we cannot guarantee that an external portal will accept a file, as portals may enforce undisclosed server-side validation rules such as proprietary DPI flags or internal file header filters.</p>
    <p>Furthermore, we are completely independent of other image tools such as Squoosh from Google Chrome Labs. While Squoosh serves as an exceptional tool for developers tuning complex image codecs, ImageTo20KB is specifically designed to solve a focused challenge: meeting strict kilobyte requirements quickly, privately, and reliably.</p>
    <h2>Supported files and format safety</h2>
    <p>ImageTo20KB accepts JPG, JPEG, PNG, and WebP image files. To prevent silent file corruption or unexpected behavior, unsupported formats—including animated GIFs, vector SVGs, and Apple HEIC photos—are rejected immediately with clear error explanations. We strongly advise users to retain their unedited original source images, as lossy compression permanently discards fine visual data.</p>
    <h2>Accessibility and universal access</h2>
    <p>Web tools should be universally accessible. Our interface is built with responsive layout grids, high-contrast light and dark modes, visible keyboard focus indicators, screen reader ARIA live-region announcements, and full support for reduced-motion operating system preferences. We continuously refine our markup to adhere to modern web accessibility principles. If you encounter any usability barrier, please notify us through our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>
    <h2>Maintaining a clean, ethical web presence</h2>
    <p>ImageTo20KB operates without mandatory user registration, subscriptions, or intrusive third-party advertising scripts. With your explicit consent, we utilize privacy-focused Google Analytics solely to monitor overall site traffic and guide technical improvements. Your image data is never accessible to analytics trackers. We minimize network payload by delivering lightweight static HTML and lazily loading processing modules only when an image is selected.</p>
    <h2>User feedback and ongoing development</h2>
    <p>Direct user feedback is essential for maintaining accuracy. If you encounter a portal with unique sizing specifications or an image that fails to compress cleanly, we welcome your detailed bug reports. Please include your device type, browser version, and specific target size when writing to us.</p>
    <h2>Where to start</h2>
    <p>Begin your compression task using our dedicated <a href="/compress-image-to-20kb/">compress image to 20KB</a> tool or our <a href="/compress-image-to-100kb/">compress image to 100KB</a> page. For custom kilobyte limits, visit the <a href="/image-compressor/">image compressor</a>, or consult our <a href="/faqs/">frequently asked questions</a> for in-depth guidance.</p>
    <p>Site Operator: {OPERATOR_NAME}, {OWNER_ROLE}.</p>
    <p>Explore our tools: <a href="/image-compressor/">Image Compressor</a> · <a href="/image-resizer/">Image Resizer</a> · <a href="/how-it-works/">Technical Architecture</a></p>
  </article></div>
</Base>
